(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-gi-quiz-version-5"]={"title":"GI Physiology — Quiz 4 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Neurocrine GI signaling molecules (neurotransmitters of the ENS) are released from neurons and diffuse to a nearby target. Which pairing of an excitatory and an inhibitory ENS neurocrine is correct?","choices":["Excitatory: nitric oxide and serotonin; Inhibitory: substance P","Excitatory: VIP and nitric oxide; Inhibitory: acetylcholine","Excitatory: acetylcholine and substance P; Inhibitory: VIP and nitric oxide","Excitatory: gastrin and motilin; Inhibitory: somatostatin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acetylcholine and substance P (along with serotonin) are excitatory ENS neurocrines, while VIP and nitric oxide are inhibitory. — GI Physiology, Slides 257-258, 268-269"},{"q":"Within the layered wall of the GI tract, the muscularis mucosae is located...","choices":["between the longitudinal and circular muscle layers, alongside the myenteric plexus","between the submucosa and the mucosa proper","outside the circular muscle layer","as the outermost layer of the gut wall"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reading from outer to inner, the order is serosa, longitudinal muscle, myenteric plexus, circular muscle, submucosa, submucosal plexus, muscularis mucosae, then mucosa — so the muscularis mucosae sits just deep to the submucosa, bordering the mucosa. — GI Physiology, Slide 6"},{"q":"Peristalsis, unlike some other reflexes discussed in GI physiology, is notable because it occurs throughout the esophagus, stomach, and small and large intestine and...","choices":["is purely a hormonal reflex requiring no nervous input whatsoever","requires the myenteric plexus but does not require the autonomic nervous system","cannot occur unless both the myenteric plexus and ANS are fully intact","requires the autonomic nervous system but not the myenteric plexus itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Peristalsis requires an intact myenteric plexus but can occur without input from the extrinsic autonomic nervous system, reflecting the enteric nervous system's capacity for independent control. — GI Physiology, Slide 326-327"},{"q":"The 'Law of the Gut' describes the pattern seen when a bolus distends the intestine: contraction occurs orad (behind the bolus) while receptive relaxation occurs downstream. This pattern is stimulated by the parasympathetic nervous system through an effect that is...","choices":["blocked by atropine, acting on muscarinic (M3) receptors on smooth muscle cells","entirely independent of receptor type since it is purely mechanical distension","blocked selectively by beta-blockers acting on adrenergic receptors instead","mediated by nicotinic, not muscarinic, receptors on smooth muscle cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The PNS-driven contraction of the Law of the Gut is mediated by muscarinic (M3) receptor activation on smooth muscle cells, and this effect is blocked by the muscarinic antagonist atropine. — GI Physiology, Slide 39"},{"q":"H2 receptor blockers such as cimetidine and ranitidine reduce gastric acid secretion because histamine, acting through H2 receptors, is one of the three major stimulants of parietal cell acid secretion — the other two being...","choices":["acetylcholine and gastrin","secretin and cholecystokinin","insulin and glucagon","motilin and somatostatin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Parietal cell acid secretion is classically driven by three converging stimulants: histamine (H2 receptor), acetylcholine (muscarinic receptor), and gastrin — so blocking any one pathway, such as H2 blockers do, reduces but does not eliminate total acid output. — GI Physiology, Slide 178"},{"q":"Between meals, cholecystokinin's action on the gallbladder and sphincter of Oddi during a fatty meal is essentially reversed, such that the gallbladder...","choices":["actively pumps bile back into the liver between meals","is bypassed entirely, with all bile going directly to the duodenum at all times","remains relaxed/filling while the sphincter of Oddi stays closed","stays maximally contracted at all times, even between meals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Between meals (low CCK), the gallbladder is relaxed and slowly fills with bile diverted from the liver, while the sphincter of Oddi remains closed, so bile is stored rather than released into the duodenum. — GI Physiology, Slides 117-118"},{"q":"Compared to haustration, mass movements of the colon are better described as...","choices":["a purely voluntary process under direct cortical control, exerted through the motor cortex and corticospinal pathways","contractions confined entirely to the region proximal to the ileocecal valve and terminal ileum","strong, sweeping contractions that propel colonic content a substantial distance toward the rectum a few times per day","continuous, slow mixing contractions occurring throughout the entire day with minimal net movement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mass movements are powerful, propulsive contractions (often triggered by the gastrocolic reflex) that move colonic contents a considerable distance toward the rectum, typically occurring only a few times daily — distinct from the slow, local mixing of haustration. — GI Physiology, Slide 61"},{"q":"Rectal distension initiates the intrinsic defecation reflex through afferent signals that spread via the myenteric plexus specifically to the...","choices":["small intestine only, sparing the colon and rectum entirely from myenteric involvement","stomach, triggering a reverse gastrocolic reflex that inhibits distal colonic motility","descending and sigmoid colon and rectum, producing peristaltic contractions that force feces toward the anus","submucosal plexus of the duodenum and proximal jejunum, sparing the myenteric plexus entirely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Afferent signals from rectal distension spread through the myenteric plexus to the descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectum, generating peristaltic waves that push feces toward the anus as part of the intrinsic defecation reflex. — GI Physiology, Slide 521"},{"q":"Enterokinase, the brush-border enzyme that converts trypsinogen to trypsin, is located specifically on the...","choices":["exocrine pancreatic acinar cells","gastric chief cells","gallbladder epithelium","intestinal mucosal cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Enterokinase (enteropeptidase) is anchored on the brush border of intestinal mucosal cells; once it converts trypsinogen to trypsin, trypsin then autocatalytically activates additional trypsinogen and the other pancreatic zymogens. — GI Physiology, Slide 104"},{"q":"Once formed, trypsin activates additional trypsinogen molecules on its own, a process best described as...","choices":["negative feedback inhibition","receptor-mediated endocytosis","autocatalytic activation","a purely hormonal (endocrine) effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Trypsin's ability to activate more trypsinogen without needing further enterokinase is called autocatalytic activation, which rapidly amplifies the pool of active protease once the initial conversion is triggered. — GI Physiology, Slide 104"},{"q":"GI hormones are historically notable as the first hormones ever identified; secretin, discovered in 1902, was followed chronologically by which hormone next, in 1905?","choices":["Gastrin","Cholecystokinin","Motilin","GIP"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Following secretin's discovery in 1902, gastrin was identified in 1905, with cholecystokinin (1928), GIP (1969), and motilin (1970) discovered later still. — GI Physiology, Slide 10"},{"q":"Beyond the intrinsic (myenteric) defecation reflex, the process is reinforced by a spinal-cord-mediated parasympathetic reflex, which serves to...","choices":["completely replace the need for the intrinsic myenteric reflex entirely","intensify and reinforce the intrinsic reflex's peristaltic contractions","inhibit the intrinsic reflex entirely to allow indefinite voluntary postponement","operate independently of rectal distension, triggered instead by colonic filling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The parasympathetic spinal defecation reflex parallels and strengthens the intrinsic myenteric reflex, amplifying the peristaltic contractions that move feces toward the anus, converting a normally weak intrinsic reflex into a much more powerful evacuation response. — GI Physiology, Slide 521"},{"q":"Cirrhosis, by increasing resistance within the low-pressure hepatic circulation, characteristically leads to which complication described alongside the liver's circulatory function?","choices":["A drop in portal venous pressure","Immediate loss of the liver's macrophage (Kupffer cell) function","Ascites","Increased cardiac output through the liver"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because the liver's circulation is normally a low-resistance, low-pressure, high-flow system, cirrhosis-driven increases in that resistance raise portal pressure and promote fluid accumulation in the peritoneal cavity, i.e., ascites. — Ch 71 Liver, Slide 3"},{"q":"Normally, roughly what fraction of cardiac output flows through the liver, despite only a small (about 9 mmHg) pressure difference driving that flow?","choices":["Essentially 0%, since the liver receives blood only from the hepatic artery","About 30%","About 70%","About 5%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Around 30% of cardiac output flows through the liver despite only a ~9 mmHg pressure difference, reflecting the hepatic circulation's characteristically low resistance and high flow. — Ch 71 Liver, Slide 3"},{"q":"CCK's trophic effects (promoting growth) act specifically on which two tissues?","choices":["The exocrine pancreas and the gallbladder mucosa","The liver parenchyma and the renal cortex","The colon mucosa and the rectal wall lining","The gastric oxyntic mucosa and the esophagus lining"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In addition to its acute effects on enzyme secretion, gallbladder contraction, and slowed gastric emptying, CCK has trophic (growth-promoting) effects specifically on the exocrine pancreas and gallbladder mucosa. — GI Physiology, Slide 14"},{"q":"Gastric secretion is regulated by parasympathetic and sympathetic input in characteristically opposite directions: parasympathetic stimulation and sympathetic stimulation, respectively,...","choices":["increase and decrease the rate of secretion","have no effect on gastric secretion at all","both increase the rate of secretion equally","decrease and increase the rate of secretion, respectively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Parasympathetic stimulation increases the rate of gastric secretion, while sympathetic stimulation decreases (or otherwise alters) it — the same generally opposing autonomic pattern seen elsewhere in the GI tract. — GI Physiology, Slide 69"},{"q":"Mucus in the GI tract is described as having several essential properties, including being adherent (sticks to food), having body (coats well), and being...","choices":["highly acidic, actively assisting pepsin's activity","composed mainly of concentrated digestive enzymes","resistant to digestion, with buffering capacity","rapidly digested itself, needing constant replacement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Among mucus's essential properties are that it is resistant to digestion by luminal enzymes and has buffering capacity, in addition to being adherent, having body, providing low-resistance lubrication, and being self-adherent. — GI Physiology, Slide 71"},{"q":"Mastication (chewing) is functionally important because it...","choices":["is unnecessary for digestion and has no effect on nutrient absorption","breaks food into smaller particles, increasing surface area for enzymatic digestion","is controlled entirely by a spinal reflex arc, independent of any brainstem input","chemically digests starch completely before the food is even swallowed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. By mechanically reducing food to smaller particles, mastication increases the surface area exposed to digestive enzymes, facilitating more efficient chemical digestion further along the GI tract. — GI Physiology, Slide 347"},{"q":"Swallowing occurs in three sequential phases: an oral (voluntary) phase, followed by involuntary pharyngeal and esophageal phases. The pharyngeal phase specifically governs...","choices":["passage of food through the pharynx into the esophagus","transit of the bolus the entire distance from the pharynx to the stomach","voluntary formation of the food bolus in the mouth","gastric accommodation of the arriving bolus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pharyngeal phase is the brief, involuntary phase that moves the bolus through the pharynx into the esophagus (while also protecting the airway), distinct from the oral phase (voluntary bolus formation/propulsion) and the esophageal phase (bolus transit from pharynx to stomach). — GI Physiology, Slides 358-359"},{"q":"During the esophageal phase of swallowing, pressure in the body of the esophagus is characteristically...","choices":["of little relevance to esophageal motility physiology overall","always identical to the intragastric pressure below the LES","close to intrapleural pressure, i.e., subatmospheric at rest","higher than atmospheric pressure throughout the entire swallow"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Esophageal body pressure closely tracks intrapleural (intrathoracic) pressure, which is subatmospheric at rest, reflecting the esophagus's location within the thoracic cavity. — GI Physiology, Slide 364"},{"q":"Voluntary postponement of defecation is possible because the external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle innervated by the...","choices":["vagus nerve, making it purely involuntary","pudendal nerves, allowing voluntary control","myenteric plexus exclusively, with no external nerve supply","splanchnic nerves only, making it purely reflexive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Unlike the internal anal sphincter (smooth muscle under autonomic control), the external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle innervated by the pudendal nerves, giving it voluntary control and allowing defecation to be consciously postponed. — GI Physiology, Slide 62"},{"q":"Saliva begins digestion in the mouth using two enzymes: salivary alpha-amylase for starch, and which enzyme for fat?","choices":["Phospholipase","Pepsin","Pancreatic lipase","Lingual lipase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In addition to alpha-amylase beginning starch digestion, saliva contains lingual lipase, which begins a small amount of fat digestion already in the mouth. — GI Physiology, Slide 42"},{"q":"The three stages of swallowing (deglutition) are named, in order,...","choices":["Voluntary, Pharyngeal, Esophageal","Voluntary, Esophageal, Pharyngeal","Oral, Gastric, Esophageal","Pharyngeal, Voluntary, Esophageal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Swallowing proceeds through a Voluntary stage (initiating the swallow), then the involuntary Pharyngeal stage (passage through the pharynx into the esophagus), and finally the Esophageal stage (passage from pharynx to stomach). — GI Physiology, Slide 43"},{"q":"Gastrin's trophic effect on the gastric mucosa is clinically illustrated by the fact that after antrectomy (removal of the gastrin-producing antrum), the remaining gastric mucosa atrophies, an effect that can be prevented by...","choices":["administering exogenous gastrin","increasing dietary fat intake","administering exogenous secretin","administering exogenous CCK"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because gastrin itself is trophic to the oxyntic mucosa, giving exogenous gastrin after antrectomy can prevent the atrophy that otherwise follows loss of the antrum's endogenous gastrin supply. — GI Physiology, Slide 11"},{"q":"Haustral contractions, the colon's mixing movements, are structurally and functionally notable in that they...","choices":["appear and disappear roughly every 30–60 seconds and cause minimal net propulsion","occur only within the transverse colon segment, nowhere else in the colon","are permanent, unchanging contractions that persist for hours without cycling","produce the majority of the colon's forward propulsive force each day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Haustral contractions cycle on a roughly 30–60 second basis, mixing colonic contents to facilitate fluid and electrolyte absorption while producing minimal forward propulsion — distinct from the more powerful, propulsive mass movements. — GI Physiology, Slide 63"},{"q":"Sympathetic innervation to the rectum and anus arises from spinal levels...","choices":["C3–C5","T10–L2","S2–S4 exclusively","T1–T4 exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sympathetic input to the rectum/anus arises from spinal cord levels T10 through L2, distributed via the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia to the proximal colon and the inferior mesenteric ganglion/hypogastric plexus to the distal colon, rectum, and anal canal. — GI Physiology, Slide 62"},{"q":"Salivary alpha-amylase, though inactivated once gastric acid drops the pH low enough, actually continues acting for a time within a swallowed bolus, digesting starch to what approximate extent by the time it reaches the stomach?","choices":["About 5% digestion occurs in the mouth, rising to as much as 40% in the stomach before inactivation","Salivary amylase is inactivated instantly upon swallowing and contributes essentially 0% of digestion","100% of starch digestion is completed by salivary amylase before the bolus is swallowed","Salivary amylase only becomes active in the small intestine, contributing 0% in the mouth or stomach"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Only around 5% of starch digestion occurs during the brief time food is in the mouth, but salivary amylase remains active within the bolus after swallowing and can achieve up to 40% digestion in the stomach before gastric acid finally inactivates it. — GI Physiology, Slide 123"},{"q":"Unlike starch, which begins luminal digestion via amylase, the disaccharides lactose and sucrose are digested...","choices":["by salivary amylase in the mouth, identically to starch","by pancreatic amylase in the intestinal lumen, identically to starch","entirely within the stomach by pepsin","only at the brush border, with no luminal digestion step"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lactose and sucrose have no luminal digestive enzyme acting on them; their digestion (by lactase and sucrase, respectively) occurs exclusively at the intestinal brush border. — GI Physiology, Slide 123"},{"q":"Protein digestion to free amino acids occurs across three sequential locations: the intestinal lumen (stomach and small intestine), the brush border, and finally the...","choices":["gastric lumen exclusively, with nothing left to digest afterward","portal blood, after absorption is already complete","gallbladder, where remaining peptides are digested by bile","cytoplasm of the mucosal cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After luminal digestion by pepsin (stomach) and pancreatic endopeptidases/exopeptidases (small intestine), and further breakdown by brush-border oligopeptidases and dipeptidases, the final step of protein digestion to free amino acids occurs within the cytoplasm of the intestinal mucosal cell. — GI Physiology, Slide 127"},{"q":"Vagal stimulation of gastrin release from G-cells is not direct — it is mediated through a specific neurocrine intermediary, which is...","choices":["gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP)","duodenal cholecystokinin (CCK)","circulating plasma motilin","pancreatic and duodenal secretin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vagal input stimulates the release of gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP), which then directly stimulates the G-cell to release gastrin — an indirect pathway, alongside the vagus also inhibiting somatostatin (which itself would otherwise inhibit gastrin release). — GI Physiology, Slide 90"}]};
